{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And you shall sanctify them today and tomorrow</b>. Rashi explained: and you shall designate them. And so is the view of Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 19:10). And similar to it: 'I have commanded my consecrated ones' (Isaiah 13:3), and likewise: 'Sanctify yourselves for tomorrow' (Numbers 11:18). And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 19:10) said that they should wash in water. But what is the reason for <b>today and tomorrow</b>, when they need to wash only once? And the correct interpretation is that they should be holy — that they not approach a woman or any impurity, for one who guards himself from impurity is called 'consecrated,' as He said concerning the priests: 'He shall not defile himself for the dead' (Leviticus 21:1), 'They shall be holy to their Elohim' (Leviticus 21:6), and it is written: 'For the priests had not sanctified themselves' (II Chronicles 30:3), meaning they had not purified themselves, and likewise: 'But the vessel of the young men is consecrated' (I Samuel 21:6) — it being known that they washed in water, and this is learned from the washing of the garments. And so they said in the Mekhilta (here): there is no washing of garments in the Torah that does not require immersion."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:10:49.334941957Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}